'''East Coast''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The electorate first existed from 1871 to 1893, and was recreated in 1999. The current MP for East Coast is [[Dana Kirkpatrick]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], who has held office since 2023.

==Population centres==
The electorate's main centres are [[Gisborne, New Zealand|Gisborne]] (32,529) and [[Tolaga Bay]] in the [[Gisborne Region]]; and [[Ōpōtiki]] and [[Whakatane]] (18,800) in the eastern part of the [[Bay of Plenty Region]]. Smaller communities in the electorate include [[Maketu]], [[Kawerau]], [[Murupara]], [[Minginui]] and [[Edgecumbe]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/east-coast-electorate-profile/ |title=East Coast – Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 21 September 2025}}</ref> Wairoa, the northernmost town in the [[Hawke's Bay (region)|Hawke's Bay]] region, was excluded by the 2007 boundary changes.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/963A871F-285E-44D8-B29D-DAFB636BCD21/78290/EastCoast_profile_1.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=4 September 2011 |archive-date=22 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110522183602/http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/963A871F-285E-44D8-B29D-DAFB636BCD21/78290/EastCoast_profile_1.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.stats.govt.nz/methods_and_services/access-data/tables/subnational-pop-estimates.aspx |title=Subnational population estimates tables – Statistics New Zealand |access-date=31 July 2009 |archive-date=28 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110828094252/http://www.stats.govt.nz/browse_for_stats/population/estimates_and_projections/subnational-pop-estimates-tables.aspx |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==History==
The East Coast electorate was first established for the [[5th New Zealand Parliament|5th Parliament]] in [[1871 New Zealand general election|1871]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=157}} [[William Kelly (New Zealand politician)|William Kelly]] was the first elected representative; he held the seat until the end of the term in 1875.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=107}}

The "most sensational electoral contest ever held in the East Coast" electorate was held in January [[1875–1876 New Zealand general election|1876]], when mysterious pieces of cardboard were distributed by supporters of [[George Read (New Zealand politician)|George Read]] in Gisborne, which hotel bars accepted as legal tender. Read, [[George Morris (New Zealand politician)|George Morris]] and Kelly received 215, 206 and 185 votes, with another candidate coming a distant fourth. Morris petitioned against Read's election. A parliamentary committee of enquiry determined that Read had not broken any laws by approving the initiative, but the House of Representatives resolved that Read was to be unseated in favour of Morris, which happened later in 1876.<ref>{{DNZB |first1=George |last1=Read |id=1r2 |author2=Philip Whyte}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=East Coast Election Report |url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=DSC18760823.2.21.1 |publisher=Daily Southern Cross| volume=XXXII| issue=5261|page=3 |date=23 August 1876 |access-date=4 September 2011 }}</ref> This was the last election enquiry held by a parliamentary committee. Subsequently, these enquiries were held by the courts.{{sfn|Mackay|1949 |p=354 }}

At the next election in [[1879 New Zealand general election|1879]], Morris was defeated by [[Allan McDonald (New Zealand politician)|Allan McDonald]], who held the electorate until he resigned in 1884.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=111}}

[[Samuel Locke (politician)|Samuel Locke]] won the resulting [[1884 East Coast by-election|by-election]] and was confirmed a few months later at the [[1884 New Zealand general election|1884 general election]]. He served until the end of the term of the [[9th New Zealand Parliament|9th Parliament]] in 1887.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=110}}

[[William Lee Rees]] stood unsuccessfully in this and subsequent by-elections and elections.

[[Andrew Graham (politician)|Andrew Graham]] won the [[1887 New Zealand general election|1887 general election]]. He resigned in 1889 before the end of the term.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=96}} [[Alexander Creighton Arthur]] won the resulting [[1889 East Coast by-election|1889 by-election]]. Arthur and Kelly (the electorate's first representative in 1871) contested the [[1890 New Zealand general election|1890 general election]], and Kelly was successful by a small margin, with 1022 to 1008 votes in his favour.{{sfn|Mackay|1949 |p=355}} He served until the end of the term in 1893,{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=107}} after which the electorate was abolished, and was replaced by the [[Bay of Plenty (New Zealand electorate)|Bay of Plenty]] and [[Waiapu (New Zealand electorate)|Waiapu]] electorates.

The electorate will be renamed to [[East Cape (New Zealand electorate)|East Cape]] for the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].<ref>{{cite web |date=8 August 2025 |title=Electorate boundaries finalised |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/electorate-boundaries-finalised/// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref>

===Members of Parliament===
From 1871 to 1893, the electorate was represented by seven Members of Parliament. When the electorate was abolished the then current MP, [[William Kelly (New Zealand politician)|William Kelly]] contested and won the new seat of {{NZ electorate link|Bay of Plenty}}.
In 1999, the electorate was recreated from most of the {{NZ electorate link|Mahia}}, and part of the {{NZ electorate link|Bay of Plenty}} electorates. Since 1999 it has been represented by two MPs.

